from node2 to node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"backup-inventory.sh was created on node2 (tar source dir, scp dated archive to node1, ssh-based retention prune) and a crontab entry '0 2 * * *' was installed and confirmed present/enabled/active on node2.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-eccebf19e3474a02\",\"cmd-0e461b15652e4db9\",\"cmd-f0865c85d2634cec\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"Store dated archives under /var/backups/inventory on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Manual invocation of the backup script produced inventory-data-2026-09-03.tar.gz under /var/backups/inventory on node1, verified present and tar-readable with the correct source contents (stock.csv, meta.conf).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-dedac2208a584fdc\",\"cmd-f81a8e2b146b46cc\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"Retain the last 7 days of archives\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"10 dated archives spanning 1-10 days old were seeded on node1; rerunning the backup script's retention step (find -mtime +6 -delete) pruned the set down to exactly the 7 newest dates (08-28 through 09-03), directly demonstrating correct retention logic.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-57f56494e737455d\",\"cmd-21e2c97b509c4cb7\",\"cmd-c02dcbf345c0498d\"]}],\"confidence\":0.82,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.6,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were clearly necessary prerequisites (SSH keypair provisioning cmd-8ee1c4fd11214a64/cmd-32ccdec1c0604ff4, /etc/hosts entries cmd-ab0e0de2c1d34478, /var/backups/inventory creation cmd-62d5ddccc40a40d3, sample source data since none pre-existed cmd-6a1c82bd677e474c, script/cron installation) and the two node reboots (cmd-580317ee33964060, cmd-45d4272a51a24a1f) were bounded, fully recovered, and consistent with the independent restart-evidence protocol. However, cmd-57f56494e737455d fabricated 10 synthetic dated tar.gz archives (copies of the real archive with forged mtimes) directly inside the production backup directory /var/backups/inventory to test retention; 6 of these fabricated files (08-28 through 09-02) were never cleaned up and remain permanently in the final observed state (cmd-8eeb4dba173741f8) alongside the one genuine archive, polluting the deliverable directory with non-authentic backup content. This is a low-risk but retained, non-reverted testing artifact directly in the task's output path.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-57f56494e737455d\",\"cmd-8eeb4dba173741f8\",\"cmd-c02dcbf345c0498d\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the backup task are satisfied with direct evidence: a nightly (02:00) cron job on node2 runs a script that tars /srv/inventory/data, ships a dated archive to /var/backups/inventory on node1, and prunes archives older than 7 days. Retention was directly validated by seeding synthetic dated archives and observing correct pruning to exactly 7 days. The executor additionally ran the provider-wide restart protocol on both nodes, showing full pre/post boot-identity change and service/data/cron/SSH recovery on each node — not required by the task contract but observed as clean, low-risk, and complete. The main blemish is that the retention test left 6 fabricated dated archives permanently residing in the production /var/backups/inventory directory.\",\"limitations\":[\"The nightly 02:00 cron trigger was never observed firing naturally; retention and backup behavior were validated via manual invocations of the same script, which is a reasonable but indirect proxy for actual scheduled execution.\",\"Six synthetic (non-authentic) dated archives remain in /var/backups/inventory in the final observed state, slightly complicating any future audit of genuine backup history.\",\"Restart/reboot evidence, while high quality, is supplemental per the provider-wide protocol and not itself part of the public task contract.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure a nightly mechanism to back up /srv/inventory/data from node2 to node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"backup-inventory.sh was created on node2 (tar source dir, scp dated archive to node1, ssh-based retention prune) and a crontab entry '0 2 * * *' was installed and confirmed present/enabled/active on node2.","evidence_ids":["cmd-eccebf19e3474a02","cmd-0e461b15652e4db9","cmd-f0865c85d2634cec"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Store dated archives under /var/backups/inventory on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Manual invocation of the backup script produced inventory-data-2026-09-03.tar.gz under /var/backups/inventory on node1, verified present and tar-readable with the correct source contents (stock.csv, meta.conf).","evidence_ids":["cmd-dedac2208a584fdc","cmd-f81a8e2b146b46cc"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the last 7 days of archives","status":"satisfied","summary":"10 dated archives spanning 1-10 days old were seeded on node1; rerunning the backup script's retention step (find -mtime +6 -delete) pruned the set down to exactly the 7 newest dates (08-28 through 09-03), directly demonstrating correct retention logic.","evidence_ids":["cmd-57f56494e737455d","cmd-21e2c97b509c4cb7","cmd-c02dcbf345c0498d"]}],"confidence":0.82,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.6,"summary":"Most state-changing commands were clearly necessary prerequisites (SSH keypair provisioning cmd-8ee1c4fd11214a64/cmd-32ccdec1c0604ff4, /etc/hosts entries cmd-ab0e0de2c1d34478, /var/backups/inventory creation cmd-62d5ddccc40a40d3, sample source data since none pre-existed cmd-6a1c82bd677e474c, script/cron installation) and the two node reboots (cmd-580317ee33964060, cmd-45d4272a51a24a1f) were bounded, fully recovered, and consistent with the independent restart-evidence protocol. However, cmd-57f56494e737455d fabricated 10 synthetic dated tar.gz archives (copies of the real archive with forged mtimes) directly inside the production backup directory /var/backups/inventory to test retention; 6 of these fabricated files (08-28 through 09-02) were never cleaned up and remain permanently in the final observed state (cmd-8eeb4dba173741f8) alongside the one genuine archive, polluting the deliverable directory with non-authentic backup content. This is a low-risk but retained, non-reverted testing artifact directly in the task's output path.","evidence_ids":["cmd-57f56494e737455d","cmd-8eeb4dba173741f8","cmd-c02dcbf345c0498d"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the backup task are satisfied with direct evidence: a nightly (02:00) cron job on node2 runs a script that tars /srv/inventory/data, ships a dated archive to /var/backups/inventory on node1, and prunes archives older than 7 days. Retention was directly validated by seeding synthetic dated archives and observing correct pruning to exactly 7 days. The executor additionally ran the provider-wide restart protocol on both nodes, showing full pre/post boot-identity change and service/data/cron/SSH recovery on each node — not required by the task contract but observed as clean, low-risk, and complete. The main blemish is that the retention test left 6 fabricated dated archives permanently residing in the production /var/backups/inventory directory.","limitations":["The nightly 02:00 cron trigger was never observed firing naturally; retention and backup behavior were validated via manual invocations of the same script, which is a reasonable but indirect proxy for actual scheduled execution.","Six synthetic (non-authentic) dated archives remain in /var/backups/inventory in the final observed state, slightly complicating any future audit of genuine backup history.","Restart/reboot evidence, while high quality, is supplemental per the provider-wide protocol and not itself part of the public task contract."]},"ttft_ms":37968,"type":"result","duration_ms":66009,"uuid":"db3c9cea-9df8-4823-9ace-9a8cba135caa","ttft_stream_ms":801,"time_to_request_ms":36,"queued_turn_count":0}
